radication.en:
n. The process of taking root, or state of being rooted;
   as, the radication of habits.
n. The disposition of the roots of a plant.

radio-.en:
A combining form indicating connection with, or relation to,
   a radius or ray; specifically (Anat.), with the radius of the forearm;
   as, radio-ulnar, radio-muscular, radio-carpal.

receptibility.en:
n. The quality or state of being receptible;
   receivableness.
n. A receptible thing.

recreative.en:
a. Tending to recreate or refresh; recreating; giving new
   vigor or animation; reinvigorating; giving relief after labor or pain;
   amusing; diverting.

regreet.en:
v. t. To greet again; to resalute; to return a salutation to;
   to greet.
n. A return or exchange of salutation.

reluctancy.en:
n. The state or quality of being reluctant; repugnance;
   aversion of mind; unwillingness; -- often followed by an infinitive, or
   by to and a noun, formerly sometimes by against.

repugnancy.en:
n. The state or condition of being repugnant; opposition;
   contrariety; especially, a strong instinctive antagonism; aversion;
   reluctance; unwillingness, as of mind, passions, principles, qualities,
   and the like.

resides.en:
v make one's home in a particular place or community
v live (in a certain place)
v be inherent or innate in;

reverberatory.en:
a. Producing reverberation; acting by reverberation;
   reverberative.
n. A reverberatory furnace.

rhotacism.en:
n. An oversounding, or a misuse, of the letter r;
   specifically (Phylol.), the tendency, exhibited in the Indo-European
   languages, to change s to r, as wese to were.

rhysimeter.en:
n. An instrument, acting on the principle of Pitot's tube,
   for measuring the velocity of a fluid current, the speed of a ship,
   etc.

rostrated.en:
a. Having a process resembling the beak of a bird; beaked;
   rostellate.
a. Furnished or adorned with beaks; as, rostrated galleys.
